<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>International news from the Americas - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24’s coverage of the political, social and economic issues in the United States, Canada, Mexico, Argentina, Brazil and across the Americas.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/</link><lastBuildDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 06:19:11 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Americas</category><title>Trump targets tax-free diesel as Republicans approach midterms</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261006-trump-targets-tax-free-diesel-as-republicans-approach-midterms</link><description>
President Donald Trump on Monday signed an executive order to ease limits on tax-free red-dyed diesel as fuel prices put pressure on Republican candidates. Trump said the move would save farmers millions of dollars and lower the cost of goods, though analysts said the change would not benefit farmers already using tax-exempt fuel.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/94f6472e-b2f4-11f1-95ba-61f6f55d211c/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-16T171401Z-792338523-RC2GKNAAXGZM-RTRMADP-3-GLOBAL-OIL.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/94f6472e-b2f4-11f1-95ba-61f6f55d211c/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-16T171401Z-792338523-RC2GKNAAXGZM-RTRMADP-3-GLOBAL-OIL.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">25071bd0-c133-11f1-8eeb-b7688360cd4b</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 03:08:02 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/94f6472e-b2f4-11f1-95ba-61f6f55d211c/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-16T171401Z-792338523-RC2GKNAAXGZM-RTRMADP-3-GLOBAL-OIL.jpg">© Mike Blake, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Trump approves firing-squad execution of Fort Hood shooter Nidal Hasan</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261006-trump-approves-firing-squad-execution-of-fort-hood-shooter-nidal-hasan</link><description>
President Donald Trump approved a firing squad execution on Monday for Nidal Hasan, the US Army psychiatrist convicted of killing 13 people at Fort Hood in 2009. Hasan's execution will be the first carried out by the US military since 1961, with the date and location yet to be set by the Army. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bbca748a-c120-11f1-aa58-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26278710455420.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bbca748a-c120-11f1-aa58-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26278710455420.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dd2f1700-c122-11f1-9d82-73264975e557</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 01:31:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bbca748a-c120-11f1-aa58-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26278710455420.jpg">© Tom Brenner, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Quebec votes as separatist surge poses test for Canada PM</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/quebec-votes-as-separatist-surge-poses-test-for-canada-pm</link><description>
Quebec separatists eyed a return to power in Canada's majority French-speaking province Monday in an election that could test Prime Minister Mark Carney's message of national unity to confront the United States. Carney is popular in Quebec, and the possible success of the separatist Parti Quebecois (PQ) is not seen as a repudiation of his stance toward President Donald Trump, whose trade war and annexation threats have infuriated Canadians. FRANCE 24's Christopher Guly reports from Ottawa.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6765e0f8-c104-11f1-80cd-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-230532-230908-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6765e0f8-c104-11f1-80cd-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-230532-230908-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6c9d89f4-c104-11f1-81ad-35a08bdbbf02</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 21:36:57 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>New York declares statewide disaster emergency as measles cases rise</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261005-new-york-declares-statewide-disaster-emergency-as-measles-cases-rise</link><description>
New York Governor Kathy Hochul declared a statewide disaster emergency on Monday as a measles outbreak spreads through under-immunised rural communities, with 108 cases reported this year. The order expands who can administer MMR vaccines and comes as neighbouring Pennsylvania battles an outbreak that has killed five people.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/02395a9e-a3b7-11f1-a97a-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-08-25T154655Z-2128226906-RC2HGJAI572K-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-USA-MEASLES.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/02395a9e-a3b7-11f1-a97a-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-08-25T154655Z-2128226906-RC2HGJAI572K-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-USA-MEASLES.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f3d851a8-c102-11f1-a541-611ce93e9845</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 21:23:04 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/02395a9e-a3b7-11f1-a97a-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-08-25T154655Z-2128226906-RC2HGJAI572K-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-USA-MEASLES.jpg">© Jayla Whitfield-Anderson, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Concerns of US interference weigh on Brazil vote</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/concerns-of-us-interference-weigh-on-brazil-vote</link><description>
Conservative Senator Flavio Bolsonaro pulled off a surprise on Sunday in the first round of Brazil’s presidential election by finishing ahead of the incumbent left-wing President Lula, whom he will face in the runoff on October 25. Suspicions of U.S. interference have been hanging over the campaign, even as Donald Trump has never hidden his closeness to the far-right former president Bolsonaro - Flavio’s father - and his animosity toward Lula da Silva. FRANCE 24's Emerald Maxwell reports.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b9203174-c022-11f1-976d-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-03T232301Z-477957309-RC2NVNACK7S9-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION-BOLSONARO.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b9203174-c022-11f1-976d-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-03T232301Z-477957309-RC2NVNACK7S9-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION-BOLSONARO.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b1afff62-c0fb-11f1-9352-21bdc74dae07</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 20:37:01 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>Return of the Bolsonaros: Why the far-right surge in Brazilian elections?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261005-return-of-the-bolsonaros-why-the-far-right-surge-in-brazilian-elections</link><description>
Do voters care about attempted coups? Brazil's Flavio Bolsonaro on the cusp of unseating Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva in the October 25th presidential runoff, Bolsonaro whose father was jailed over the Brazilian version of the January 6th storming of the US Capitol. Up and down the ballot, there are other Bolsonaros elected in the first round as the far-right surges. Is it about incumbent fatigue? Do Brazilians subscribe to Bolsonaro’s "God, Homeland, Family" mantra?
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2dd48b3a-c0d4-11f1-9f3e-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T022837Z-1703279203-RC2OWNAV3T5S-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2dd48b3a-c0d4-11f1-9f3e-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T022837Z-1703279203-RC2OWNAV3T5S-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2ae7219e-c0e8-11f1-8398-d7b2867a1f02</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 18:44:35 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Quebec separatists vow independence vote after provincial election win</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261005-separatists-win-quebec-election-with-pledge-to-vote-on-leaving-canada</link><description>
The separatist Parti Québécois won provincial elections on Monday, but fell just short of enough seats to govern alone in Canada's French-speaking province, Quebec. The victory comes just two weeks ahead of a vote in Alberta, Canada's economic powerhouse, on whether it wants to begin the process of leaving the country. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ee67e000-c12d-11f1-869f-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/6b830d89698e660c56483528be3b402dbf9d4724.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ee67e000-c12d-11f1-869f-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/6b830d89698e660c56483528be3b402dbf9d4724.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0c0d31d4-c0d7-11f1-9cbe-8509acbc916e</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 16:08:47 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ee67e000-c12d-11f1-869f-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/6b830d89698e660c56483528be3b402dbf9d4724.jpg">© Andrej Ivanov, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>How Lula’s successes in Brazil may have paved the way for his presidential defeat</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261005-how-lulas-successes-in-brazil-may-have-paved-the-way-for-his-presidential-defeat</link><description>
Brazil’s veteran leftist President Luiz Inacio "Lula" da Silva trailed Flavio Bolsonaro, the son of his far-right predecessor, in the first-round presidential vote on Sunday. Some analysts suggest that the sweeping anti-poverty measures Lula put in place over two decades may have helped give rise to a growing lower-middle class drawn to the far right’s promise to get the government out of people’s lives.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b44c1f6a-c0c1-11f1-8f3f-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T051605Z-1725054154-RC2OWNAEUWVT-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b44c1f6a-c0c1-11f1-8f3f-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T051605Z-1725054154-RC2OWNAEUWVT-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">21e5e520-c0c1-11f1-88e0-851ca5bd765a</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 15:21:10 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b44c1f6a-c0c1-11f1-8f3f-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T051605Z-1725054154-RC2OWNAEUWVT-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION.jpg">© Brian Snyder, Reuters</source><dc:creator>Paul MILLAR</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'Very bad result for Lula: Bolsonaro is far more effective at speaking to that growing middle class'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/very-bad-result-for-lula-bolsonaro-is-far-more-effective-at-speaking-to-that-growing-middle-class</link><description>
Eve Irvine is pleased to welcome Felipe Krause, the head of Oxford University's Brazilian Studies Programme. Brazil's election reflects a political landscape that has become increasingly polarized, explains Krause. And he describes the current mood as one of “tired polarization: People are just exhausted. They feel that the political class is just kind of offering more of the same". The first round reveals a significant consolidation of the Brazilian right in government. Brazil's expanding middle class is increasingly attracted to entrepreneurialism and individualism, constituencies that "Bolsonarismo" has proved particularly effective at reaching.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/02676028-c091-11f1-a552-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-080339-081250-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/02676028-c091-11f1-a552-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-080339-081250-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">07b1760e-c091-11f1-981a-6b7cf4bcc737</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 12:54:49 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Perspective</category><title>Brazil presidential election: 'Bolsonaro being ahead is a surprise,' researcher says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261005-brazil-presidential-election-bolsonaro-being-ahead-is-a-surprise-researcher-says</link><description>
Far-right candidate Flavio Bolsonaro has pulled off a surprise win in the first round of Brazil’s presidential election. He defied most polls to win with 47 percent, compared to 45 percent for leftist incumbent Luiz Inacio Lula de Silva. We spoke to Thomas Zicman de Barros, a research fellow at CEVIPOF, the political research centre at Sciences Po University, and columnist for our sister radio station RFI. He told us that never before in Brazilian history has a candidate who was behind in the first round then succeeded in the second.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1adb6bc6-c0ba-11f1-8052-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-15118627956ac39b00d5d4f1-95598905.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1adb6bc6-c0ba-11f1-8052-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-15118627956ac39b00d5d4f1-95598905.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">12dd0672-c098-11f1-a16a-97a620563070</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 12:52:32 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Stuart NORVAL</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Family of jailed ex-president Bolsonaro scores big in Brazil as son heads to run-off</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261005-family-members-of-jailed-ex-president-jair-bolsonaro-win-big-in-multiple-brazil-polls</link><description>
Family members of former Brazilian president Jair Bolsonaro won big in Sunday's general election, including the right-wing lawmaker's eldest son Flavio Bolsonaro, who surpassed President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva in the first-round presidential vote. During the campaign, Flavio Bolsonaro promised to secure amnesty for his jailed father. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/449179c4-c088-11f1-8ee6-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26277433106719.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/449179c4-c088-11f1-8ee6-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26277433106719.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">36f3ccd4-c085-11f1-bffa-33bcee93007a</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 07:06:54 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/449179c4-c088-11f1-8ee6-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26277433106719.jpg">© Lucas Landau, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Brazil presidential election heads to run-off as Flavio Bolsonaro leads Lula</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261005-brazil-presidential-election-runoff-bolsonaro-lula</link><description>
Right-wing Senator Flavio Bolsonaro pulled off a surprise first-place finish ahead of President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva in Sunday's election, which heads to a run-off vote on October 25 as neither clinched a majority.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/629cda5a-bfe6-11f1-b662-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C9LQ3ZQ.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/629cda5a-bfe6-11f1-b662-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C9LQ3ZQ.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">388d4672-c050-11f1-8426-b34b733c314a</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 00:16:05 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/629cda5a-bfe6-11f1-b662-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C9LQ3ZQ.jpg">© Evaristo Sa, Mauro Pimentel, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title>Flavio Bolsonaro: A profile of Brazil's potential president</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261004-a-profile-of-brazil-s-potential-president</link><description>
FRANCE 24's Gavin Lee speaks to Brazil analyst Henrique Tavares Furtado about how far-right presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro is pledging to reshape the country, and asks: who is the man behind the political persona?
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d73b2380-c01d-11f1-8efa-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/9e6d0d4bbdec1f8c7bb91b10df86b261d1bb19d0-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d73b2380-c01d-11f1-8efa-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/9e6d0d4bbdec1f8c7bb91b10df86b261d1bb19d0-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e0148166-c01a-11f1-9188-e39ea6268944</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 18:34:51 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Gavin LEE</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Brazil heads to the polls for high-stakes election between Lula and Flavio Bolsonaro</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/brazil-heads-to-the-polls-for-high-stakes-election-between-lula-and-flavio-bolsonaro</link><description>
Brazilians began casting their votes on Sunday in a closely watch presidential election pitting incumbent President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva against right-wing Senator Flavio Bolsonaro, son of former president Jair Bolsonaro. Nearly 160 million voters will also pick lawmakers, senators and governors in an election that will determine whether Latin America's biggest nation follows a regional swing to the right championed by US President Donald Trump. France24 International affairs commentator Douglas Herbert shares further insights.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ff70d622-c010-11f1-baeb-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-170357-170917-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ff70d622-c010-11f1-baeb-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-170357-170917-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">043da7fc-c011-11f1-af99-15ecb37b46be</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 16:38:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Woman at centre of Cornell gang rape lawsuit targeted with threats, lawyer says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261004-woman-at-center-of-cornell-gang-rape-allegations-targeted-with-threats-jane-doe</link><description>
The woman who alleged that she was drugged and raped by seven male students at a Cornell University fraternity house has been the target of threats, her lawyer told CNN on Sunday. Cornell's president earlier voiced support for an external review of the Ivy League institution facing widespread outcry over its handling of the case.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/daa775ba-c00d-11f1-86df-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-02T154814Z-1310255220-RC24TNATK0SJ-RTRMADP-3-USA-CRIME-CORNELL.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/daa775ba-c00d-11f1-86df-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-02T154814Z-1310255220-RC24TNATK0SJ-RTRMADP-3-USA-CRIME-CORNELL.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">00a2646e-c00e-11f1-88df-87f1effa807d</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 16:25:01 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/daa775ba-c00d-11f1-86df-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-02T154814Z-1310255220-RC24TNATK0SJ-RTRMADP-3-USA-CRIME-CORNELL.jpg">© Lauren Petracca, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Brazil: Lula and Bolsonaro tied in polls</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/brazil-lula-and-bolsonaro-tied-in-polls</link><description>
Brazilians began casting their votes on Sunday in a closely watch presidential election pitting incumbent President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva against right-wing Senator Flavio Bolsonaro, son of former president Jair Bolsonaro. Mara Nogueira, Senior Lecturer in Urban Geography at Birbeck, University of London, says the tight race reflects the consolidation of two political camps, with many Brazilians voting not out of support for a candidate, but to keep the other out of power. She points to the cost of living, religion and social media echo chambers as key factors shaping the vote, alongside a major banking scandal that has so far had limited impact on Flavio Bolsonaro’s campaign.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/148e8480-bff5-11f1-baa5-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-140634-141113-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/148e8480-bff5-11f1-baa5-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-140634-141113-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">192b2bba-bff5-11f1-a72c-d5f3666ace29</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:19:54 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Lula de Silva: 80-year-old incumbent Brazilian president seeks 4th term</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/lula-de-silva-80-year-old-incumbent-brazilian-president-seeks-4th-term</link><description>
Brazilians began casting their votes on Sunday in a closely watch presidential election pitting incumbent President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva against right-wing Senator Flavio Bolsonaro, son of former president Jair Bolsonaro. Nearly 160 million voters will also pick lawmakers, senators and governors in an election that will determine whether Latin America's biggest nation follows a regional swing to the right championed by US President Donald Trump. France 24 correspondent Jan Onoszko tells us the latest from Rio de Janeiro.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a972198-bff4-11f1-ad27-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-140151-140634-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a972198-bff4-11f1-ad27-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-140151-140634-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7f88d7e6-bff4-11f1-a9b2-3f124670dd82</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:17:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Lula and Bolsonaro face off in Brazil presidential race</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/lula-and-bolsonaro-face-off-in-brazil-presidential-race</link><description>
Twelve candidates are on the ballot, but the presidential race is centred on two rivals: President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and Flavio Bolsonaro. Lula is seeking a fourth and final term, warning that Brazil’s sovereignty is at stake amid pressure from Donald Trump. Bolsonaro, meanwhile, has highlighted his ties to the White House, saying Brazil needs better deals with the US, including over its rare mineral resources. Nick Rushworth has more on the controversial senator. Dr Andrea Aruska de Souza Santos, Director of King's College Brazil Institute in London, shares further insights.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4af16eb0-bfe7-11f1-a8ca-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-130852-131819-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4af16eb0-bfe7-11f1-a8ca-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-130852-131819-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5019f81c-bfe7-11f1-a747-936a5daf9f74</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 11:56:08 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Cornell president breaks silence over alleged campus rape</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/cornell-president-breaks-silence-over-alleged-campus-rape</link><description>
Cornell University remains at the centre of controversy over an alleged gang rape at a fraternity on campus, with the university president now breaking his silence. In an eight-minute video statement released Saturday night, Michael Kotlikoff called the allegations, made by a woman known as Jane Doe, deeply disturbing and said the university must do better. Shirli Sitbon has this report.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/22935d1c-bfe6-11f1-95ff-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-130412-130611-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/22935d1c-bfe6-11f1-95ff-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-130412-130611-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">27ab91a2-bfe6-11f1-894f-1dfb2b076328</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 11:53:09 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Polls close in Brazil's high-stakes election pitting Lula against Flavio Bolsonaro</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261004-brazilians-start-voting-in-tight-election-between-lula-and-flavio-bolsonaro</link><description>
Brazilians voted Sunday in a high-stakes presidential election pitting incumbent Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva against far-right challenger Flavio Bolsonaro, following a campaign dominated by corruption scandals, economic anxiety and accusations of US meddling. The knife-edge election comes at a moment when the region is shifting to the right in line with the Trump administration.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e99df506-bff1-11f1-b070-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D27G68K.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e99df506-bff1-11f1-b070-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D27G68K.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">39db38ba-bfe5-11f1-976b-199a82b6f5af</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 11:17:45 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e99df506-bff1-11f1-b070-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D27G68K.jpg">© Sergio Lima, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Brazil: A portrait of Flavio Bolsonaro</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/brazil-a-portrait-of-flavio-bolsonaro</link><description>
Brazilians are voting for governors and lawmakers, but the biggest stakes are in the presidential race. Twelve candidates are on the ballot, but the contest is seen as a toss-up between President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and Flavio Bolsonaro. Lula is seeking a fourth and final term, warning that Brazil’s sovereignty is at stake amid pressure from Donald Trump. Bolsonaro, meanwhile, has highlighted his ties to the White House, saying Brazil needs better deals with the US, including over its rare mineral resources. Nick Rushworth has more on the controversial senator.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c6f86b4a-bfd7-11f1-914f-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-110148-110322-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c6f86b4a-bfd7-11f1-914f-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-110148-110322-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">cb7ede7e-bfd7-11f1-bb56-257e158da7e1</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 09:49:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'General divide' in Brazil's elections: 'Voters not particularly enthusiastic about either of them'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/brazil-polls-open-in-a-few-hours</link><description>
Alison Sargent is pleased to welcome Dr Marieke Riethof, Senior Lecturer in Latin American Politics at University of Liverpool. She sees parallels with the Brazil's 2022 election even if Flávio Bolsonaro does not have the same charisma or mobilizing capacity as his father. His programme remains a hardline political agenda that focuses on security and a reduction in the role of government. Lula, by contrast, offers a more interventionist vision, particularly in areas such as education, and socioeconomic policy. It could weaken multilateralism and reshape the country’s relationships with the United States, China, the European Union and the BRICS nations.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c2d762a2-bfcb-11f1-8523-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-090829-091808-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c2d762a2-bfcb-11f1-8523-69fad0a7cb9f/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-090829-091808-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c7c929ee-bfcb-11f1-8f07-3d6ebccde61b</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 08:25:26 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Flock's AI tracking sparks privacy fears and violence</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/flock-s-ai-tracking-sparks-privacy-fears-and-violence</link><description>
In the US, Flock Security cameras form an AI-powered surveillance network that records licence plates and other vehicle information. Their controversial use has become a political issue ahead of the midterm elections, and the cameras are increasingly being targeted by vandals. Caitlin Brown explains.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5bdbd99c-bfcc-11f1-bd77-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-095423-095630-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5bdbd99c-bfcc-11f1-bd77-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261004-095423-095630-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">60ad241c-bfcc-11f1-a5fd-57b3c23b77bb</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 08:23:37 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Ossoff and El-Sayed: Two Senate races and what they say about the Democratic Party</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261004-ossoff-and-el-sayed-two-senate-races-and-what-they-say-about-the-democratic-party</link><description>
Senator Jon Ossoff, a Democratic favourite, is running for re-election in Georgia in the 2026 US midterms while Abdul El-Sayed is a Senate candidate in the battleground state of Michigan. The two Democrats have very different profiles and platforms, underscoring divergences within the party. They could also determine the party's future ahead of the 2028 presidential election. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/64a85de8-bcd4-11f1-9439-816a8398162d/w:1024/p:16x9/main-ossof-sayed.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/64a85de8-bcd4-11f1-9439-816a8398162d/w:1024/p:16x9/main-ossof-sayed.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a1ef0102-bcd4-11f1-a8da-8f92b66c42e9</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 07:21:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/64a85de8-bcd4-11f1-9439-816a8398162d/w:1024/p:16x9/main-ossof-sayed.png">© Graphic studio FMM (composite)</source><dc:creator>Leela JACINTO</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Lula and Bolsonaro make final push for votes ahead of Brazil's knife-edge election</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261003-lula-and-bolsonaro-make-final-push-for-votes-ahead-of-brazil-s-knife-edge-election</link><description>
Brazil's President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and his main challenger Flavio Bolsonaro wrapped up their campaigns on Saturday in the powerhouse state of Sao Paulo, where a fifth of the nation's vote is based, seeking to rally supporters and convince the undecided on the eve of Sunday's nail-biter election.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4ae32c90-bf62-11f1-bcb8-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-03T181839Z-337218467-RC2QVNAP2RHU-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION-LULA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4ae32c90-bf62-11f1-bcb8-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-03T181839Z-337218467-RC2QVNAP2RHU-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION-LULA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7139ccce-bf61-11f1-a668-8511c6a54966</guid><pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 20:16:01 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4ae32c90-bf62-11f1-bcb8-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-03T181839Z-337218467-RC2QVNAP2RHU-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION-LULA.jpg">© Amanda Perobelli, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Lula and Bolsonaro in final push for votes ahead of Brazil's knife-edge election</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261003-lula-and-bolsonaro-in-final-push-for-votes-ahead-of-brazil-s-knife-edge-election</link><description>
Brazil's President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and his main challenger Flavio Bolsonaro made their final pitch to voters Saturday with rallies in the pivotal state of Sao Paulo ahead of a nail-biter election that has been marred by scandals and mudslinging.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/60b7c81e-bf62-11f1-9ca8-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261003-201231-201430-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/60b7c81e-bf62-11f1-9ca8-578a4b968fd6/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261003-201231-201430-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">65dba982-bf62-11f1-9966-5d29e392e04d</guid><pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 20:10:15 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Tennessee prison chief who oversaw botched Christa Pike execution resigns</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261003-tennessee-prison-chief-who-oversaw-botched-christa-pike-execution-resigns</link><description>
The head of Tennessee's prison system has resigned in the first major shake-up since the stunning failed execution of convicted murderer Christa Pike, who is in critical condition after surviving two lethal injections. Tennessee Governor Bill Lee, who previously denied Pike clemency, has halted further executions through the rest of the year while a review is conducted.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0b87e52e-bf4c-11f1-8e0a-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26225608672619.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0b87e52e-bf4c-11f1-8e0a-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26225608672619.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e77f5a06-bf4e-11f1-ac59-ed2bb6d17f7f</guid><pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 17:24:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0b87e52e-bf4c-11f1-8e0a-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26225608672619.jpg">© Mark Humphrey, AP archives</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Flavio Bolsonaro: In the name of the father, sons, and holy ideology</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261003-flavio-bolsonaro-in-the-name-of-the-father-sons-and-holy-ideology</link><description>
The first round of Brazil’s presidential election on Sunday pits two well-known names in the political ring: Lula vs. Bolsonaro. But it’s not former president Jair Bolsonaro facing off against the incumbent head of state – it’s his eldest son, Flavio Bolsonaro. He’s less charismatic than his father but just as ideologically radical as the family patriarch.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cf0647a4-bce3-11f1-aaab-ddce071186a9/w:1024/p:16x9/flaviobolso.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cf0647a4-bce3-11f1-aaab-ddce071186a9/w:1024/p:16x9/flaviobolso.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4c5f4a8a-be5f-11f1-9cb7-435d07bfc5a0</guid><pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 09:48:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cf0647a4-bce3-11f1-aaab-ddce071186a9/w:1024/p:16x9/flaviobolso.jpg">© Amanda Perobelli, Reuters</source><dc:creator>Sébastian SEIBT</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Brazil’s Lula gears up for ‘last dance’ with run for fourth presidential term</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261002-brazil-s-lula-gears-up-for-last-dance-with-run-for-fourth-presidential-term</link><description>
President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva will stand for re-election in Brazil on Sunday. At 80, the former steelworker hopes to capitalise on his popularity and economic successes to win a fourth term in office but is neck-and-neck in the polls with far-right rival Flavio Bolsonaro.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ea92c66-b9f0-11f1-8cc7-91cf9f3cd539/w:1024/p:16x9/WEB-IVS-NOT-26SEP-PORTADA-WEB.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ea92c66-b9f0-11f1-8cc7-91cf9f3cd539/w:1024/p:16x9/WEB-IVS-NOT-26SEP-PORTADA-WEB.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ba316d26-be6a-11f1-a33c-cb0d77449df6</guid><pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 16:20:06 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ea92c66-b9f0-11f1-8cc7-91cf9f3cd539/w:1024/p:16x9/WEB-IVS-NOT-26SEP-PORTADA-WEB.png">© AP</source><dc:creator>Guido BURDMAN</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Brazil: Who is presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261002-brazil-who-is-presidential-candidate-flavio-bolsonaro</link><description>
He was still relatively unknown in Brazil just a few months ago. The 45-year-old senator is now neck and neck with incumbent President Lula in the polls for the upcoming presidential election. Flavio Bolsonaro's victory would add Brazil to the long list of Latin American countries that have shifted to the right or far right in recent months.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0d68ed50-be6d-11f1-a887-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-Flavio-Bolsonaro-thumb0.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0d68ed50-be6d-11f1-a887-13420ab900a2/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-Flavio-Bolsonaro-thumb0.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2f0d093c-be6d-11f1-9b4e-97a041700218</guid><pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 15:06:54 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
